    "message": {
     "content": "### Verdict: Do Not Accept As-Is; Negotiate\n\nThis is a **standard baseline L6 offer**, but it is **not compelling enough to accept as-is**. \n\nBecause Google recruited you while you are happy at Meta, you hold strong leverage. Taking this offer in its current state gives you almost no financial upside, exposes you to onboarding/transition risk, and offers zero transition compensation (no sign-on bonus).\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown vs. Current Meta Comp\n\n| Component | Meta (IC6 PM) | Google Offer (L6 PM) | Delta / Assessment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Below your current base) |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Initial Equity / yr** | ~$250,000 (avg) | ~$270,000 (annualized) | **+$20,000** (See vesting note below) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | **$0** | **$0** (Major missing lever) |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+5% (Essentially flat)** |\n\n#### Key Factors to Note:\n1. **The \"Refresh Stacking\" Illusion:** The recruiter's illustration of refresh stacking is how Google models long-term compensation, but **Meta does the exact same thing**. At Meta IC6, you also receive annual stacked refreshers. Assuming Google refreshes will outpace Meta\u2019s to bridge the gap is not a guaranteed calculation.\n2. **Vesting Schedule Structure:** If Google applies its common front-loaded schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%), your Year 1 and Year 2 equity would be ~$356,400, boosting Year 1 TC to ~$638k. However, Years 3 and 4 drop significantly (a \"pay cliff\") unless heavily offset by performance refreshes. If the offer uses a flat 25%/year schedule, Year 1 is only ~$552k.\n3. **No Risk Premium:** Moving from Meta IC6 to Google L6 without a 15\u201320% bump in guaranteed/expected Year 1 compensation or a sizable sign-on bonus does not justify resetting your internal brand, performance history, and project momentum.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nGoogle typically enters with standard mid-band numbers and expects negotiation from candidates at L6+ coming from peer Tier-1 companies.\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Counter Request | Target Outcome |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$260,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $255,000 (Matches/beats current base) |\n| **Equity (4-yr GSU)**| $1,080,000 ($270k/yr)| **$1,300,000** ($325k/yr) | $1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000** | $40,000 \u2013 $60,000 |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | ~$552,000 | **~$680,000+** | **~$620,000 \u2013 $640,000** |\n\n---\n\n### How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone or email and frame the conversation around three pillars:\n1. **You weren\u2019t looking:** You enjoy your current team and scope at Meta.\n2. **Current compensation baseline:** Emphasize that taking a lower base salary and virtually flat Year 1 total compensation makes the move financially lateral.\n3. **Unvested equity / Transition costs:** Highlight that you are walking away from an established track record and ongoing refresh pipeline at Meta.\n\n#### Sample Negotiation Script:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the scope of the role. However, looking at the total package, it doesn\u2019t quite make financial sense for me to leave Meta yet.*\n>\n> *I\u2019m currently at $240k base with ~$526k in total comp, along with an established refresh cadence and unvested equity. The current offer represents a slight drop in base salary and is roughly flat on Year 1 total compensation.*\n>\n> *Because I\u2019m very happy in my current role, I would need to see a meaningful step up to make the transition. If we can get closer to **$260,000 base**, an initial equity grant of **$1.3M over 4 years**, and a **$75,000\u2013$80,000 sign-on bonus** to bridge the unvested equity and bonus transition, I would be ready to sign immediately.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation\n\n* **Do not accept** the initial offer.\n* **Do not walk away yet**\u2014negotiate firmly. Google recruiters expect pushback on first-round L6 offers.\n* If Google meets you near **$250k base + $1.2M equity + $50k sign-on**, it becomes a strong, risk-adjusted package to make the switch. If they refuse to move and hold at flat comp, stay at Meta.",
